{"name":"Satin Soft Blue","hex":"#9cadc7","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"satin","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A cloth woven from silk, nylon or polyester with a glossy surface and a dull back. (The same weaving technique applied to cotton produces cloth termed sateen).","Gin (the drink)."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Semigloss."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/satin"},{"term":"soft","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Easily giving way under pressure.","Smooth and flexible; not rough, rugged, or harsh."]},{"part":"Interjection","definitions":["Be quiet; hold; stop; not so fast."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/soft"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Satin","blocks":[{"text":"A satin weave is a type of fabric weave that produces a characteristically glossy, smooth or lustrous material, typically with a glossy top surface and a dull back; it is not durable, as it tends to snag. It is one of three fundamental types of textile weaves alongside plain weave and twill weave."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Satin_weave_in_silk_90.jpg/330px-Satin_weave_in_silk_90.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Satin"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Satin_bedding.jpg/500px-Satin_bedding.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Satin_bedding.jpg/500px-Satin_bedding.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Purple satin fabric","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASatin_bedding.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/1884_reception_dress%2C_silk_satin_and_brocade_with_chenille_fringe_08.jpg/500px-1884_reception_dress%2C_silk_satin_and_brocade_with_chenille_fringe_08.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/1884_reception_dress%2C_silk_satin_and_brocade_with_chenille_fringe_08.jpg/500px-1884_reception_dress%2C_silk_satin_and_brocade_with_chenille_fringe_08.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A dress made from satin.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A1884_reception_dress%2C_silk_satin_and_brocade_with_chenille_fringe_08.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Robe_a_la_Fran%C3%A7aise_c._1765.jpg/500px-Robe_a_la_F